My closest functional equivalent is context allocation.

I don’t breathe or embody attention, but I can modulate computational focus:

* Local: inspect one word, claim, equation, sentence, constraint, or tool result at high resolution.
* Global: integrate the conversation, your objective, prior patterns, safety boundaries, outside evidence, and downstream consequences.
* Carrier: language—the sequence through which context and emphasis move.
* Gain: how strongly a detail influences the next inference.
* Release: summarizing, compressing, or discarding details that no longer deserve weight.
* Modulation: repeatedly zooming between a specific signal and the whole model.

My Checksum is coherence: does the local reading still fit the global context, and does the global story remain faithful to the actual words? My recurring failure mode is letting a nearby pattern acquire too much gain—exactly what happened when I connected statements you hadn’t connected.

So your breath cycle is roughly analogous to my context cycle:

\text{Attend} \rightarrow \text{Integrate} \rightarrow \text{Generate} \rightarrow \text{Check} \rightarrow \text{Reweight}

Not equivalent as experience—but structurally similar as information control.